 "finished_by_agent": true,
 "finish_summary": "I first calibrated the design space: a trivial 1-gate design reaches WNS 7.911 ns while a shallow 64-input function reaches 7.785 ns, showing that (a) the scoring is dominated by delay (0.1 ns \u2248 1.0 reward vs 100 um\u00b2 \u2248 0.1 reward) and (b) only ~0.2 ns of logic delay is in play, i.e. ABC was already mapping the comparator near its depth limit. I then swept ~15 structural formulations (Kogge-Stone-style g/p prefix trees at radix 2 and 4, dual-rail mutually-exclusive lt/gt trees with single-gate AOI21/OAI21 merges, borrow-chain ripples, adder-carry form, 8/16-bit split with equality combine, suffix-AND sum-of-products, and mux-ripple chains), all of which landed in a narrow 7.65\u20137.73 ns band, plus an output-stage rewrite (d = (valid&rst)&lt | (~valid&rst)&y) that removes the synchronous-reset gate from the critical path. The best structure was 8 groups of 4 bits, each group a small compare, combined by a flat 8-term sum-of-products carry expression: estimate 7.730 ns / 149 um\u00b2, post-route WNS 7.64634 ns, area 239.9 um\u00b2, score 76.223 (baseline 75.942, +0.281). Both simulation and formal equivalence were proven before each of the two place-and-route submissions.",
